      "anti_example": "Not 'está un baño aquí' for 'there's a bathroom here': say 'hay un baño aquí'. Estar is for where a known thing is."
    },
    {
      "term": "usted",
      "category": "Pronoun",
      "definition": "Polite 'you' for one person: strangers, older people, clients, your doctor.",
      "example": "Disculpe, ¿usted es la doctora López? — Excuse me, are you Dr. López?",
      "discussion": "It takes the same verb form as él and ella: usted tiene, not usted tienes. With a stranger, start with usted."
    },
    {
      "term": "poder",
      "category": "Verb",
      "definition": "Expresses 'can': being able to, being allowed to, and asking for things.",
      "example": "¿Puedes pasarme la sal? — Can you pass me the salt?",
      "discussion": "¿Me puede traer la cuenta? is the normal polite way to ask a waiter. Add por favor and you're set."
    },
    {
      "term": "ir a + infinitivo",
      "category": "Pattern",
      "definition": "Talks about plans and what's coming soon: voy a comer, vamos a ver.",
      "example": "Mañana voy a llamar a mi mamá. — Tomorrow I'm going to call my mom.",
      "discussion": "In everyday talk this is the normal way to speak about the future. You'll hear it far more than the textbook future."
    },
    {
      "term": "tener",
      "category": "Verb",
      "definition": "Covers 'have', and also age, hunger, thirst and cold, where English uses 'be'.",
      "example": "Tengo mucha hambre, ¿comemos algo? — I'm really hungry, shall we eat?",
      "anti_example": "Not 'soy 30 años' for 'I'm 30': say 'tengo 30 años'. Same with 'tengo frío' for 'I'm cold'."
    },
    {
      "term": "no … nada",
      "category": "Pattern",
      "definition": "Gives you 'nothing' or 'not anything': no goes before the verb and nada after it.",
      "example": "No quiero nada más, gracias. — I don't want anything else, thanks.",
      "anti_example": "Not 'quiero nada' or 'no quiero algo': Spanish needs both words, no and nada."
